{"name":"Palatinate Blue","hex":"#273be2","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"palatinate","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["The office or rank of a palatine.","A territory ruled by a palatine."]},{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Of or pertaining to a palatinate or the Palatinate."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/palatinate"},{"term":"blue","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Of a blue hue.","Depressed, melancholic, sad."]},{"part":"Noun","definitions":["The colour of the clear sky or the deep sea; the colour midway between green and violet in the visible spectrum and one of the primary additive colours.","Anything coloured blue, especially to distinguish it from similar objects differing only in colour."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/blue"}],"entries":[{"title":"Palatinate (colour)","blocks":[{"text":"Palatinate or palatinate purple is a purple colour associated with Durham University and the County and City of Durham. The term has been used to refer to a number of different shades of purple. The Oxford English Dictionary defines it as a \"light purple or lavender colour\", which is used for Durham academic hoods. For corporate purposes Durham University uses a darker shade."}],"link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Palatinate_(colour)","wikidata":"Q7126798"},{"title":"Palatinate (region)","blocks":[{"text":"The Palatinate, or the Rhenish Palatinate (Rheinpfalz), is a historical region of Germany. The Palatinate occupies most of the southern quarter of the German state of Rhineland-Palatinate (Rheinland-Pfalz), covering an area of 5,450 km2 (2,100 mi2) with about 1.4 million inhabitants. Its residents are known as Palatines (Pfälzer)."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/34/Teilbereiche_der_Pfalz.png/330px-Teilbereiche_der_Pfalz.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Palatinate_(region)","wikidata":"Q326359"},{"title":"Blue","blocks":[{"text":"Blue is one of the three primary colours in the RGB (additive) colour model, as well as in the RYB colour model. It lies between violet and cyan on the spectrum of visible light. The term blue generally describes colours perceived by humans observing light with a dominant wavelength between approximately 450 and 495 nanometres. The clear daytime sky and the deep sea appear blue because of an optical effect known as Rayleigh scattering. An optical effect called the Tyndall effect explains blue eyes. Distant objects appear more blue because of another optical effect called aerial perspective."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/5a/Earth-in-true-color-himawari-09.jpg/330px-Earth-in-true-color-himawari-09.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Blue","wikidata":"Q1088"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/ce/Chancellor_Bill_Bryson_at_Degree_Ceremony_Durham_University.jpg/500px-Chancellor_Bill_Bryson_at_Degree_Ceremony_Durham_University.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/ce/Chancellor_Bill_Bryson_at_Degree_Ceremony_Durham_University.jpg/500px-Chancellor_Bill_Bryson_at_Degree_Ceremony_Durham_University.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"The Durham BSc hood, \"Palatinate silk, bound with white fur, and with a scarlet band half-an-inch wide next to the fur\".[11]","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AChancellor_Bill_Bryson_at_Degree_Ceremony_Durham_University.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/86/Durham_University_Racecourse.JPG/500px-Durham_University_Racecourse.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commo